"""shadow-netprivacy — encrypted DNS and MAC address policy of Shadow OS.

    shadow-netprivacy status [--json]
    shadow-netprivacy dns {dot|dot-strict|doh|off} [--provider P] [--dnssec D]
    shadow-netprivacy mac {per-boot|per-network|hardware}
    shadow-netprivacy mac-exception {add|remove} <NetworkManager connection UUID>
    shadow-netprivacy boot

DNS (systemd-resolved is the only resolver; NetworkManager hands it the DNS
servers of every network):
  dot          DNS-over-TLS to the provider when it answers on port 853,
               plain DNS to the same provider otherwise (the default)
  dot-strict   DNS-over-TLS only: no name resolves when port 853 is blocked
  doh          DNS-over-HTTPS through dnscrypt-proxy (Debian package, opt-in)
               on 127.0.2.1:53, chained behind systemd-resolved
  off          the DNS servers of the network you are connected to
  --provider   quad9 (default) | mullvad | cloudflare |
               custom:<ip>#<tls name>[,<ip>#<tls name>...] (dot, dot-strict)
  --dnssec     allow-downgrade (default) | yes | no
In every mode except off, all names go to the provider and none, in plain
text, to the network's own DNS server, which only answers for the network's
own names (resolved's Domains=~.).

MAC address (NetworkManager; nothing is reconnected, a network takes its new
address the next time it connects):
  per-boot     a private address per network, new at every boot (the default)
  per-network  a private address per network that stays across boots
  hardware     the device's real address
  mac-exception add <uuid>  this network keeps one private address across
               boots (connection.stable-id=${CONNECTION} on that profile)

Files: the shipped defaults are /usr/lib/systemd/resolved.conf.d/
80-shadow-privacy.conf and /usr/lib/NetworkManager/conf.d/80-shadow-privacy.conf.
This program writes only /etc/systemd/resolved.conf.d/90-shadow-user.conf and
/etc/NetworkManager/conf.d/90-shadow-mac.conf, and in doh mode
/etc/shadow-os/dnscrypt-proxy.toml with the dnscrypt-proxy.service drop-in
/etc/systemd/system/dnscrypt-proxy.service.d/50-shadow-netprivacy.conf.

Safety properties:
  * a DNS change is verified against what systemd-resolved reports after the
    restart; if resolved does not come back with it, the previous file is put
    back and the command fails;
  * doh mode is switched on only after dnscrypt-proxy has answered a query;
  * the kernel parameter shadow.netprivacy=off turns all of it off for one
    boot (shadow-netprivacy.service runs "shadow-netprivacy boot", which
    writes overrides into /run): the network's own DNS servers and the
    devices' own MAC addresses are used;
  * "status" reports what systemd-resolved and NetworkManager have in force,
    never only what was asked for, and runs for any user;
  * nothing about lookups or networks is logged.

Graphical tools call this program through pkexec (polkit action
org.shadow.netprivacy.manage); every command but "status" must run as root.
"""
